A||||13||Silver88GT||A question for those up North||||5||
Z||000000||Silver88GT||12-05-2001||12:08 AM||||My garage is unheated, but attached to the house, so it rarely gets below freezing.  My question is "how cold is too cold to do things like, specifically, replacing bushings?"  Does it even matter?<P>I worry about bolts snapping, etc?  This is my first project car.  I always just wheeled my project bikes into the warm basement, but the Fiero won't fit through the doorway.    <P>Just wondering what all you that have winters far worse than southern Indiana do.<P>Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Silver88GT (Wingless)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mach10||12-05-2001||12:12 AM||||If it's cold enough that you have to worry about your fingers and toes going numb... IT'S TOO COLD FOR ALL BUT THE MOST URGENT MECHANICS  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000002||scarydok||12-05-2001||12:14 AM||||I'm from St. Louis, but we do have pretty cold winters here...I have never had a problem doing work in an unattached garage, and I would think that an attached garage would be warmer, so the answer to your question is I think it would be ok to do that type of work in your garage.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||12-05-2001||12:19 AM||||My fieros have allways goten to say in an unheated garage. I have worked on them when its snowing out, without wind its really not that bad. A space heater allways helps too.<P>Whenever im working on my fiero I get excited, so I stay warm  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Scott||12-05-2001||01:17 AM||||Get one those smaller floor-mount style space heaters.  You can use it to warm yourself, your tools, and the area of the car you're working on.  They're very nice, espaecially on very cold days. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Posthumane||12-05-2001||02:54 AM||||Ah, brings back memories of sitting in my mom's unheated garage with my jacket and gloves on cleaning my engine whilst seeing my breath. Oh wait, that was last week!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||2farnorth||12-05-2001||06:11 AM||||I changed the engine in one of mine when the temp was -12.  It was inside an unheated garage.  I had to go in the house and warm my hands and toes frequently.  If I'm doing body work I take the panel off and take it to my heated basement.<P>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000007||Nashco||12-05-2001||08:41 AM||||I've rebuilt an engine at 5* F, not fun, but I had to do it.  You can work on your car at whatever temperature, as long as you're willing to hang out in the cold.  Some things I've learned are to keep as many tools as possible in the house, at least all your hand tools.  If you leave them in the cold, they are hard to work with because your hands get so numb.  Also, if you have any fluids (engine oil in particular) you're changing, keep the new stuff in the house and very warm.  If you try to pour cold oil, it not only takes forever, but also takes a long time to get through the oiling system.<P>Have fun <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  Just remember to keep lots of cocoa/coffee on tap!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||12-05-2001||08:51 AM||||The cold won't have any affect on metal parts. Plastic gets a little more brittle but won't usually cause any problems, just be a little more careful when removing plastic stuff.<P>Some sort of heat is great. I have a kerosene heater that will heat the whole garage 20 or so degrees above outside and is nice and toasty right next to it. You probably at least want something to warm your hands.<P>For poly bushings, you might want to take the stuff in the basement to put the bushings back in the sleeves. Then again, they might go in easier cold, try it and see.<P>Oh, and don't stick your tongue to any of the tools.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Silver88GT||12-05-2001||11:11 AM||||Thanks everyone.  I will make sure to stay as warm as possible.  (Halogen work lights work great as well.)<P>I've been concerned about this since breaking a bolt off in the head of my Harley a few years ago.  Haven't chanced it since then.  I guess it probably had nothing to do with the temp.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||K2m4||12-05-2001||11:40 AM||||man my kerosene heater turns my garage into a sauna. i can get my car to sweat in the dead of winter up here. and actually work on my car with a t shirt and shorts on.  I live in upstate ny were sometimes the weather can get quite frisky up here]<P><BR>kerm<BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000011||avengador1||12-05-2001||07:15 PM||||Just make sure you use a good penetrating oil like Kroil before attemptimg to remove any nuts or bolts. Let it soak overnight if possible. I did my engine last winter in an unheated attached garage. As long as the garage doors remained shut it wasn't too bad. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||mgbringo||12-05-2001||11:00 PM||||I use a Knipco kerosene heater to keep my hands warm.  Use Carhart bibs and jacket vest to stay warm.  Lay down blanket on floor when I have to lay on it.  The cold will have no effect on bolts etc.<P>I once changed a clutch in my 49 Ford Pickup outside in -20 weather.  Lots of warming up time in the house.  But it got done.<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||kneebarx||12-06-2001||12:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Master_Sushi:<BR><B>My fieros have allways goten to say in an unheated garage. I have worked on them when its snowing out, without wind its really not that bad. A space heater allways helps too.<P>Whenever im working on my fiero I get excited, so I stay warm   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hopefully, not <B>too</B> excited.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
